00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:04,618
From a Petition to a Decision At a public convention a senator

2
00:00:04,632 --> 00:00:10,500
invited residents to discuss proposed legislation One advocate wanted to repeal an

3
00:00:10,513 --> 00:00:15,395
outdated prohibition another urged the council to retain a regulation that protected

4
00:00:15,408 --> 00:00:20,776
a vulnerable group The controversy concerned both fairness and the possibility of

5
00:00:20,789 --> 00:00:26,632
unintended effects The chair asked speakers to cite evidence refrain from personal

6
00:00:26,645 --> 00:00:32,829
attacks and acknowledge a reasonable concession A coalition offered an allied campaign

7
00:00:33,079 --> 00:00:37,447
but members would not accept an ally who encouraged a riot They

8
00:00:37,461 --> 00:00:41,329
preferred to mobilize support through a peaceful rally and a clearly stated

9
00:00:41,342 --> 00:00:46,540
pledge After debate the council agreed to initiate a review and announce

10
00:00:46,553 --> 00:00:52,158
its findings annually A proposed veto was withdrawn and a subsidiary committee

11
00:00:52,171 --> 00:00:57,658
would handle technical questions The mayor would inaugurate the consultation rather than

12
00:00:57,671 --> 00:01:04,250
dictate its outcome Public participation can counter inertia when citizens turn dissatisfaction

13
00:01:04,263 --> 00:01:10,724
into a practicable proposal Strong conviction matters but persuasion also requires listening

14
00:01:10,737 --> 00:01:13,092
to people whose priorities differ from our own
